'''apophyllite''' (a-pof′i-līt or ap-ọ̄-fil′īt), ''n.'' [So named because of its tendency to exfoliate (cf. Gr. ἀποφυλλίζειν, strip of its leaves), < Gr. ἀπό, off, from, + φύλλον (= L. ''folium'', a leaf) + ''-ite''<sup>2</sup>.] A mineral allied to the zeolites, occurring in laminated masses or in tetragonal crystals, and having a strong pearly luster on the surface of perfect cleavage, parallel to which it separates readily into thin laminæ. {{smaller|It exfoliates also under the blowpipe. From its peculiar luster it is sometimes called ''ichthyophthalmite'', that is, fish-eye stone. It is a hydrated silicate of calcium and potassium, containing also some fluorin.}}

'''apophyllous''' (ap-ọ̄-fil′us), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀπό, off, from, + φύλλον = L. ''folium'', a leaf.] In ''bot.'', having distinct leaves; eleutherophyllous: applied to a perianth with distinct sepals and petals: opposed to ''gamophyllous''.

'''apophysary''' (a-pof′i-sạ̄-ri), ''a.'' [< ''apophysis'' + ''-ary''.] Having the character of an apophysis or outgrowth; apophysial.
{{smaller block|In Magas the ''apophysary'' system is composed of an elevated longitudinal septum reaching from one valve to the other.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', IV. 190.}}}}

